{#- Shared pipeline-step emission, {% import %}-ed by route_api.go.j2,
    worker.go.j2, and job.go.j2 (mirrors ciac-backend-ts's own
    "one shared macro" choice -- Python/Rust duplicate this per file
    instead; minijinja's {% import %} makes sharing simple here too).

    `result` flows through the pipeline as `any`: a `handler`/`call`
    step's output type has no static relationship to its input beyond
    "whatever the target's Handle/method declares", so `any` is the
    honest typing -- the alternative, threading a precise type through
    every step, would need a real type per intermediate value that
    doesn't exist in the IR today.

    Go has no `?`/exception propagation, and a naive shared `err`
    variable reassigned by every step runs into two dead-end shapes:
    `result, err := f()` needs `:=` for `err`'s first declaration but
    `=` for every later step (which step is "first" depends on which
    kinds are present), and pre-declaring `var err error` up front
    makes it a compile error ("declared and not used") on any pipeline
    whose only step is `publish` (nothing ever reads it) -- both
    reachable by 24UpdatePlan.md M3 examples (`kafka-pipeline.ciac`,
    `realtime-progress.ciac`). The fix: every step gets its own
    block-scoped `if v, err := ...; err != nil { <on_err> } else {
    result = v }` (or, for `publish`, a bare `if err := ...; err !=
    nil`) -- fresh names in a fresh scope every time, so step order/
    composition never matters and no shared mutable error variable is
    ever declared. `on_err` is a caller-supplied Go statement (or
    semicolon-joined statements) naming `err`, rendered as-is and
    reformatted by the real `gofmt` binary at the render_go call site.

    `call` steps render plausible code (mirroring Rust/TS's own
    M3-era templates, which did the same before their `call`
    infrastructure -- clients/scope arms -- actually existed).

    `match` was originally written the same speculative way, type-
    asserting `result` to `map[string]any` and reading the matched
    field out of it as a generic JSON value -- plausible for a step
    whose predecessor might be untyped, but never actually exercised
    by any 24UpdatePlan.md example. 28UpdatePlan.md M7d's own live
    proof against `multi-service-media.ciac` (Transcoder's `pipeline
    Transcode: TranscodeVideo -> match status { ... }`) was the first
    real run to reach this code and it panicked:
    `handle_message_once`'s payload was decoded straight into the
    typed `schemas.Video` struct (worker.go.j2's drain loop always
    knows the worker's own payload type), so `result` was never a
    `map[string]any` in the first place by the time `match` ran --
    the map assumption was simply wrong whenever `payload_type` is
    known, which for a worker or a typed-API pipeline it always is.
    Fixed to switch on `result.(payload_type).<field in PascalCase>`
    instead, the same typed-field-access shape Rust's own `match
    result.status { ... }` already uses (Rust's pipeline threads the
    real `Video` type throughout rather than `any`, so it never had
    this problem to begin with). The old `map[string]any` branch
    stays as the fallback for the one context that still has no
    known payload type -- `job.go.j2` passes `payload_type = ""`
    unconditionally, since a scheduled job has no incoming record to
    type a `match` against -- still inert, no example reaches it.

    v0.24 M4: a `handler` step whose `step.handler.is_typed_handler` is
    true is a v0.7 typed handler (a plain function --
    `logic.{ClassName}`/`services.{ClassName}`, not a
    `New{ClassName}(st).Handle` struct+constructor pair, see
    `logic.go.j2`), whose single param is always the pipeline's own
    payload record (sema-enforced: a typed handler used as a pipeline
    step is only ever reachable this way with an exact-payload-type
    param, confirmed against every 24UpdatePlan.md M4 example) -- so
    `result` (declared `any`) needs one explicit type assertion back to
    that concrete type before the call, unlike a classic handler's own
    `any`-in/`any`-out `Handle`. `payload_type` is the caller's
    precomputed Go type string (`"schemas.Order"`); empty for a step
    site with no typed payload in scope (worker/job pipelines, unused
    by any M4 example either way). -#}
{% macro emit_step(step, indent, on_err, payload_type) %}
{%- if step.kind == "handler" and step.handler.is_typed_handler %}
{{ indent }}if v, err := {{ step.handler.handler_package }}.{{ step.handler.class_name }}(ctx, st, result.({{ payload_type }})); err != nil {
{{ indent }}	{{ on_err }}
{{ indent }}} else {
{{ indent }}	result = v
{{ indent }}}
{%- elif step.kind == "handler" %}
{{ indent }}if v, err := services.New{{ step.handler.class_name }}(st).Handle(ctx, result); err != nil {
{{ indent }}	{{ on_err }}
{{ indent }}} else {
{{ indent }}	result = v
{{ indent }}}
{%- elif step.kind == "publish" %}
{{ indent }}if err := queue.PublishJSON(ctx, st.Queue, st.World, "{{ step.subject }}", result); err != nil {
{{ indent }}	{{ on_err }}
{{ indent }}}
{%- elif step.kind == "call" %}
{{ indent }}if v, err := clients.New{{ step.call.class_name }}(st).{{ step.call.method | go_pascal }}(ctx, result); err != nil {
{{ indent }}	{{ on_err }}
{{ indent }}} else {
{{ indent }}	result = v
{{ indent }}}
{%- elif step.kind == "match" %}
{%- if payload_type %}
{{ indent }}switch v := result.({{ payload_type }}); v.{{ step.field | go_pascal }} {
{%- else %}
{{ indent }}switch v, _ := result.(map[string]any)["{{ step.field }}"].(string); v {
{%- endif %}
{%- for arm in step.arms %}
{%- if arm.label %}
{{ indent }}case "{{ arm.label }}":
{%- else %}
{{ indent }}default:
{%- endif %}
{%- for inner in arm.steps %}
{{ emit_step(inner, indent ~ "\t", on_err, payload_type) }}
{%- endfor %}
{%- endfor %}
{{ indent }}}
{%- endif %}
{%- endmacro %}
